Here’s a simple RabbitMQ implementation demo using Spring Boot (traditional AMQP style).

This is the classic approach with:
- `RabbitTemplate` (producer)
- `@RabbitListener` (consumer)
- Explicit Exchange + Queue + Binding beans

Good for: monolithic apps / single service demos / learning AMQP basics.
For microservices binder style → see Spring Cloud Stream example.

This demo includes:
- Producer: sends a message to an exchange (routed to a queue)
- Consumer: listens for messages from that queue
- REST endpoint: triggers the producer


✅ Step 1: Add Maven Dependencies
In your pom.xml, add:

```xml
<dependencies>
    <!-- RabbitMQ / AMQP -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-amqp</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Needed because we expose a REST API -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>
```


✅ Step 2: Configure RabbitMQ in application.yml

```yaml
spring:
  rabbitmq:
    host: localhost
    port: 5672
    username: guest
    password: guest
```

Ensure RabbitMQ is running locally or change these values for your environment.


✅ Step 3: Create Configuration Class

```java
@Configuration
public class RabbitMQConfig {

    public static final String QUEUE = "demo_queue";
    public static final String EXCHANGE = "demo_exchange";
    public static final String ROUTING_KEY = "demo_routingKey";

    @Bean
    public Queue queue() {
        // new Queue(name) => durable=true by default (queue survives broker restart)
        return new Queue(QUEUE);
    }

    @Bean
    public TopicExchange exchange() {
        // TopicExchange is durable by default
        return new TopicExchange(EXCHANGE);
    }

    @Bean
    public Binding binding(Queue queue, TopicExchange exchange) {
        return BindingBuilder.bind(queue).to(exchange).with(ROUTING_KEY);
    }
}
```

Mental model:
Producer → Exchange (`demo_exchange`) + routing key → Queue (`demo_queue`) → Consumer


❓ Why do we use a routing key?

A routing key tells the **exchange** which queue(s) should get the message.

Producer does **not** usually pick a queue directly. It sends:
Exchange + routing key + message

Exchange compares that routing key with each queue’s **binding**, then decides where it goes.

Why it exists:
1) Decoupling — producer doesn’t need to know queue names
2) Selective delivery — one exchange, many queues, different keys
3) Flexible routing — especially with Topic (`order.*`, `order.#`)

Simple example:

```text
Exchange: order.exchange

Producer sends key = "order.created"    → email-queue   (bound to order.created)
Producer sends key = "order.cancelled"  → cancel-queue  (bound to order.cancelled)
Producer sends key = "order.paid"       → no match      → not routed
                                           (or alternate/DLX if configured)
```

Without routing key logic:
- Fanout ignores it (broadcast to all bound queues)
- Direct/Topic need it to choose the right queue(s)

Interview one-liner:
Routing key = label on the message; binding = rule on the queue; exchange matches them to route.

Note (Spring Cloud Stream / “new way”):
You often don’t set routing key yourself — binder manages exchange/queue/binding via `destination` (+ consumer `group`).
Traditional AMQP (this file) = you manage routing key explicitly.


✅ Step 4: Create the Producer

```java
@Service
public class MessageProducer {

    private final RabbitTemplate rabbitTemplate;

    public MessageProducer(RabbitTemplate rabbitTemplate) {
        this.rabbitTemplate = rabbitTemplate;
    }

    public void sendMessage(String message) {
        // Prefer RabbitTemplate (Spring Boot auto-configures it)
        rabbitTemplate.convertAndSend(
                RabbitMQConfig.EXCHANGE,
                RabbitMQConfig.ROUTING_KEY,
                message
        );
        System.out.println("Sent message: " + message);
    }
}
```

Optional (for broker-restart survival of message body):
set persistent delivery mode when publishing (deliveryMode = 2).
Queue durability alone is not enough if messages are transient.


✅ Step 5: Create the Consumer

```java
@Component
public class MessageConsumer {

    @RabbitListener(queues = RabbitMQConfig.QUEUE)
    public void receiveMessage(String message) {
        System.out.println("Received message: " + message);
        // After method returns successfully, Spring acks the message (default).
    }
}
```

Interview tip:
Default listener behavior is safer than raw auto-ack on delivery.
For critical jobs, use manual ack / error handling / DLQ patterns.


✅ Step 6: Create a REST Controller to Send Messages

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/messages")
public class MessageController {

    private final MessageProducer messageProducer;

    public MessageController(MessageProducer messageProducer) {
        this.messageProducer = messageProducer;
    }

    @PostMapping
    public ResponseEntity<String> send(@RequestParam String msg) {
        messageProducer.sendMessage(msg);
        return ResponseEntity.ok("Message sent: " + msg);
    }
}
```


✅ Step 7: Run RabbitMQ (Docker)

```bash
docker run -d --hostname rabbit --name rabbitmq \
  -p 5672:5672 -p 15672:15672 rabbitmq:3-management
```

Dashboard: http://localhost:15672  
Login: guest / guest


